**Appendix A: Staff Testimonials**

*Supporting evidence for the Digital Coach consultation response*

January 2026

The following testimonials were voluntarily provided by colleagues across different curriculum and professional service areas. They illustrate the impact of the Digital Coach role on teaching quality, staff confidence, and workload efficiency.

# Richard Browne

*Bids Team (PLS)*

Graeme supported our team in looking at potential AI solutions for increased efficiency within our everyday working practice. Graeme took the time to engage with us face-to-face, listening and asking questions to ensure he had a full understanding of what was needed. He undertook research and presented us with different options, indicated pros and cons of each, and demonstrated how we might use them. Throughout this exercise, Graeme was friendly, knowledgeable and responsive to our needs, and acted as a crucial link between us and the IT Department.

Graeme also presented an engaging session on AI during the DigiWest CPD event, covering the evolving AI landscape and best practice in applying it to our work. Overall, I would argue that this role – and Graeme's approach to it – aligns with the College's strategic approach to digital technologies and equipping our staff and students with vital skills for future career opportunities.

# Stephanie Lee

*Lecturer, Access to HE*

I have worked closely with the Digital Learning Team, and in particular with both Louise Woodhouse and Graeme Wright, who are so knowledgeable and clearly passionate about the growing number of digital tools that will help us in our planning and teaching. They are inspirational and the support I have received has had a tangible and sustained impact on my teaching practice as an Access to HE lecturer.

The guidance and coaching provided went far beyond technical troubleshooting. It supported me to think more critically and confidently about how digital tools can enhance teaching, learning and assessment in ways that are inclusive, purposeful, and learner centred. This was particularly valuable for Access to HE learners, many of whom return to education with varied digital confidence and significant barriers around access, skills, and self-belief. The whole team has made a clear difference to my ability to design learning activities that were accessible, engaging, and aligned with good pedagogical practice rather than technology for its own sake. The support has been practical, responsive, and grounded in an understanding of FE classrooms and real teaching pressures. It also helped me reflect on digital inclusion as a core aspect of quality TLA, not an add-on.

From my experience, the knowledge, experience and approach brought by the team has significant potential impact not only on individual teaching practice, but on staff confidence, consistency of learner experience, and the college's wider commitment to inclusive, high-quality education. I strongly believe this role is essential if the college is to continue developing effective, inclusive digital practice that genuinely supports both staff and learners - especially in this ever-growing digital world we live in.

# Lucy Cruse

*Lead Practitioner - Hearing and Vision, Faculty of Inclusive Practice*

I have heard the news about your team and I'm so sorry to hear it. It's a huge shock and particularly for myself working with you and your team to implement assistive technology to support inclusion and independence. It is a huge area that is developing within education, there's lots of research surrounding this ever growing need within education.

It will be a huge loss - yourself, Liam, Barry and Graeme have been invaluable, supporting me implementing technologies for learners with sight and hearing loss. Graeme has been so helpful with SharePoint, brighter futures hub and developing our SEN provision access.

I also was super excited about the accessibility tool checker that Graeme was working on and this will be such a shame if this isn't something that can be accessed.

# Jed Randall

*Maths Tutor*

Graeme has provided outstanding support in my role and has significantly contributed to my professional development and confidence in using digital tools. One of the most impactful opportunities he gave me was the chance to contribute to the DigiWest INSET on 8th July 2025. This experience not only allowed me to share ideas but also deepened my understanding of digital learning practices.

Graeme has helped me build confidence in seeking out and trying new technologies to enhance my teaching. His introduction to the 21CLD principles provided a clear pathway for me to explore innovative approaches to teaching and learning. Additionally, Graeme demonstrated how the Microsoft Learn platform could benefit both my own CPD and the wider department, which has been invaluable for ongoing development.

His delivery of essential AI training for the Maths and EMT teams was particularly insightful. It gave us practical strategies for using AI to provide feedback to learners, which has improved the quality and efficiency of our teaching. Graeme has also met with me to support my Digital Champions project, offering ideas and guidance that have helped shape its direction.

Finally, Graeme created a Teams community for Digital Champions, giving us a collaborative space to share current ideas and best practice. This has fostered a culture of innovation within the team and directly benefited our teaching and learning. Graeme's knowledge, experience and skills have had a tangible impact on my work and, in my view, are essential for driving high-quality TLA and promoting digital inclusion across the college.

# Emily Green

*TLA Manager, Quality Team*

Graeme has carried out vital work during our recent digital inclusion activities, which has already supported with swiftly identifying a number of areas that didn't yet fully meet our inclusion promise. Alongside the identification of these areas through Learner Voice, Learning Walks (LRA) and Staff Talks, Graeme has started to upskill the team to be able to provide TLA focused digital solutions which can support managers and staff on both a team and individual level. This has been vital for our work as a Quality Team, so that we can support with the delivery of Weston College's Digital Promise and staff development with a digital inclusion lens.

Through our work, Graeme has already booked in and delivered area specific CPD focused on areas of inclusion, and many managers have identified that this is an area they would like added to their QIP as a result. Graeme demonstrated high levels of confidentiality and creativity during the activities, with a strong understanding of the new Ofsted toolkit under the focus of inclusion and how this relates to digital TLA. Graeme is highly personable and approachable, and staff have expressed how supported they have felt to develop their skill-sets as a result. An example of this was when Graeme worked with an Apprenticeship team to support with developing their online induction, and improving the learner initial assessment and experience. Following this, staff members have continued to develop their digital TLA and feel more confident with supporting within their teams, and the impact will be measured through Learner Voice and Apprenticeship Surveys (current window).

# Ronnie Houselander-Cook

*Cross-College CPD*

Championing CPD for staff through the Digital Champion programme, that otherwise do not have access to meaningful progression without aiming to be a manager or course leader. Positioning himself as a relatable, understanding leader.

Consistently being a reliable point of contact to problem solve TLA challenges and provide cost effective alternative to resources that cost money eg padlet & menti.

Enthusiasm, passion and kindness that is contagious and can inspire others to give digital ago, especially when barriers to digital pedagogies are established to a knee-jerk culture.

Sharing inclusive approaches, strategies and evidence informed knowledge in COG and INSET day sessions which have been the most signed up option until the model of delivery has changed.

Overlaps of creative teaching experiences, with strong digital skills which are hard to find.

# Rachel Britton

*Sixth Form*

Graeme's ongoing delivery of education on Artificial Intelligence is an invaluable asset to the college. His teaching ensures that both staff and students remain informed and equipped to navigate the rapidly evolving digital landscape. As a staff member, I have personally benefitted from his sessions—gaining greater understanding and confidence in applying AI tools to enhance teaching, learning, and administrative efficiency.

Graeme's commitment extends beyond classroom delivery; he is always willing to engage directly with students, bringing specialist insight and practical examples that enrich their learning. He is already scheduled to visit our students at the end of January, an engagement that forms an imperative part of their learning experience in the current educational and technological climate. His involvement continues to make a significant contribution to the college's ability to deliver future-focused, relevant education.

# Megan Brookes

*Cross-College*

I have attended several CPD sessions delivered by Graeme and found them to be both engaging and highly informative. Each session has provided me with valuable ideas to enhance teaching, learning, and assessment (TLA) practices, as well as practical strategies that can be adapted to support learners. Graeme's delivery style is excellent—clear, inspiring, and easy to follow—which consistently motivates me to improve my approach.

I have also had the opportunity to work alongside Graeme, and his depth of knowledge and professionalism are exceptional. He has significantly enhanced my understanding of digital approaches and strategies, as well as how to interpret policies and processes related to digital usage, including areas such as AI.

# Maria Miles

*Sixth Form*

The Learning Technology team support our learners with running Microsoft Office qualification exams. Many learners and staff have been successful at achieving these.

The team often provide immediate digital support to staff due to the IT team being extremely busy and also not available on site at Loxton. 1-1's have also been provided to staff who are struggling with use of digital technology in the classroom.

Graeme Wright has provided digital CPD this half term on using AI for learners and staff. Staff have as a result implemented the following in their sessions:

'Used with Yr.2 to enable them to access more research more confidently without having to wade through Google scholar for hours for debate and controversy essays with the expectation that they check their chosen studies back on Google scholar - has been useful but also did go through with them about expectations, transparency of use and appropriateness of use.'

'I've run some exam papers and mark schemes through Chat CPT to generate more questions/mark schemes in the style of the exam board to give the students more range/making them unable to search for mark schemes.'

'It's good if used well. I encouraged students to use Co-pilot and ChatGPT to support their research process. I do show them examples where it needs prompting such as using credible resources and apply referencing from established journals and academics. I've also allowed them to use AI tools to generate images to support their production planning process with storyboarding and generating shot types to give them some ideas of what can be filmed, it really helped them before filming.'

# Katie Middleton - *Learning Support*

I have worked collaboratively with Graeme for the last year, the support and guidance he has offered has been invaluable and he is always trying to come up with proactive ways to support staff and learners. We are currently working on a project to support ESOL learners with their digital tech ability in order for them to be able to access the support within my team (learning support). We have established that their digital accessibility is probably the first barrier in their understanding and communication. Digital support for ESOL is an area that I feel really needs attention at the moment, to empower staff and transform learners understanding and accessibility to help them thrive in our college. Graeme has shared my views on this and has been my biggest support in driving this. This is going to be a long, working project, something that I feel my knowledge would struggle with and I need to heavily rely on Graeme.

Graeme is very understanding and passionate about supporting the most vulnerable of learners to access learning material, no matter their barriers. His knowledge and expertise is unmatched and is always someone I will turn to for support digitally.

I created the learning support and ADHD SharePoint, which has helped so many learners and staff gain more knowledge and support, only possible with the patience (very much needed) and knowledge that Graeme provided. He went above and beyond and gave me the confidence needed to launch the SharePoint. We have plans for the future to add to this and add in AI, something that will ease learning supports workload and transform the learner's experience. This feels beyond my capability or capacity and something that again I would like to entrust Graeme with.

# Kara Weymouth

*Healthcare*

Graeme plays an important and very real role in supporting teaching staff to deliver high-quality teaching, learning, and assessment through the effective use of digital tools. What sets Graeme apart is that he understands teaching first. Digital practice is a way of improving learning, not an expectation to keep up or a box to tick.

A clear example of Graeme's impact is the support he provided to the Health assessing team to develop more confident and advanced digital approaches to workshop delivery. Graeme worked closely with the team, taking the time to show them how to access relevant resources, how to build these into their sessions, and how to use learner engagement during and after workshops to check understanding. This helped assessors feel more confident adapting their delivery in the moment and using engagement as part of the assessment process, rather than relying on digital tools in a superficial way.

Graeme is very aware that staff come with different levels of digital skill and confidence. He adjusts his approach depending on who he is working with, breaking things down when needed and moving at a pace that feels supportive rather than overwhelming. This flexibility boosts confidence and helps staff feel capable, rather than exposed or behind. Importantly, Graeme makes these sessions feel approachable and enjoyable, which encourages staff to engage, ask questions, and actually try new approaches in their own practice.

The Digital Coach role is particularly important for staff who may not have the skills, experience, or time to keep up with constant changes in digital practice. Graeme provides clear signposting to the right tools and, just as importantly, shows staff how to use them in a way that makes sense for their role and subject area. This practical, hands-on support removes barriers and helps staff see the value of digital approaches in improving learner engagement and understanding.

Graeme also supports the college's wider commitment to digital inclusion by encouraging staff to think about accessibility, learner experience, and fairness when using digital tools. His approach helps ensure that digital practice supports all learners and does not create additional barriers.

In a college environment where expectations around digital delivery continue to grow, Graeme's role is essential. His ability to adapt his support, build confidence, and create a positive learning experience for staff makes a clear and lasting difference to teaching and assessment practice.

I fully support Graeme's continued role as Digital Coach and believe his contribution is vital to supporting staff, strengthening digital practice, and maintaining high-quality teaching, learning, and assessment across the college.

# Jenny Gamlin

*Practice Educator, Healthcare (HAC - Health and Counselling)*

Thinking back to January 2025 I was quite happy to just go along with crowd and do "death by PowerPoint" for workshops. I didn't particularly enjoy presenting the workshops and I don't feel that the learners got anything from them either.

Having attended DigiWest I was enthused with many ideas of how to be more inclusive and improve accessibility, while avoiding "death by PowerPoint." The question was how to do this? I contacted Graeme Wright and asked for help.

Graeme attended a meeting with the Healthcare Apprenticeship team in a bid to improve the quality of inductions and workshops and for them to be more interactive when either delivered face to face or online.

Here is where Graeme came into his own. He was full of ideas of what we could do and how we could achieve this. The fact that Graeme was not part of the healthcare team was an added bonus as he listened to exactly what we wanted and then proposed ideas.

Without the help from Graeme our new induction process would still be "clunky" and very much talking "at" learners. We now have an interactive induction that uses Padlet, Microsoft forms, Moodle training and feedback.

Since this has started I have been in regular contact with Graeme asking about how I can "tweak" certain areas of my workshops. This has led to much more interaction during sessions, very little time apologising that things are not working correctly and increased positive feedback from learners.

This feedback gives me more confidence with Digi skills and makes me want to improve my learning and the improved outcomes for learners. In this day and age we are turning more and more to online resources and learners should look forward to their workshops rather than finding them a chore.

Thanks to Graeme I feel confident to call him to ask for help, he is always there and understands that we are not all brought up in the digital era. His input into our team has led to all of us being less afraid, and more eager to embrace the technology.

# Frances Chilcott

*Digital Champion*

I have worked with Graeme in their role as Digital Coach as I had volunteered to be part of the Digital Champions initiative. This program encouraged staff to volunteer to develop advanced digital skills, not only for their own professional growth but also to support colleagues within their faculties. This fostered collaboration, improved confidence in using technology and helped embed digital best practices into everyday teaching.

What stood out most was Graeme's personal approach. He took the time to sit down with each of us individually, understand what we wanted to learn, and what we were passionate about. He then worked to identify projects that aligned with those interests while also benefiting our faculties. This tailored support made the initiative feel meaningful and relevant, and it motivated me to engage more deeply with digital tools.

Graeme also demonstrated foresight by planning to extend this initiative to support staff, which would have been a significant step toward improving digital inclusion across all areas of the college. Many staff can use computers for essential tasks, but few are aware of the wide range of tools and techniques available to save time and enhance productivity. Graeme has consistently worked to bridge this gap, ensuring that digital skills are not just a technical requirement, but a driver of quality and innovation. This was demonstrated with the recent DigiWest event that he helped put together, showcasing the different technology available and leading training sessions.

Their knowledge, experience and ability to engage staff at all levels make them an asset to the college. Retaining a Digital Coach role is essential for maintaining momentum in digital transformation and ensuring that our practices remain inclusive, efficient and aligned with the needs of both staff and learners.

# Claude Ujile

*Engineering*

I have been positively mentored by my Graeme Wright (GW) through their thoughtful and deliberate application of 21st Century Learning Design (21CLD), which has significantly enhanced my own knowledge, teaching experience, and pedagogical skill set. GW critically examined the 21CLD framework and identified a clear need to move towards a more student-centred pedagogy, recognising its potential to improve knowledge construction and foster greater student ownership of learning. Through our professional dialogue, GW emphasised the importance of a mindset shift for me and learners. One that promotes motivation, autonomy, and a deeper understanding of metacognitive regulation.

A key focus of our discussions was the development of students' self-regulation skills, particularly through interactive and deep learning experiences aligned with the 21CLD components. GW articulated a strong belief that these approaches, applied consistently, allow learners to be dynamic and support them in "being where they want to be" in their learning journey. I recognised that my interaction and deep learning over time helped learners with the toolkit required to recognise and correct misconceptions and to become more comfortable when learning feels challenging. This strongly aligned with my own teaching philosophy, which I often express to learners as learning how to "figure it out like a scientist" by curiosity, critical thinking, and resilience in the face of uncertainty.

Our discussions also explored the concept of energy and effort in learning, using the analogy of a battery. GW challenged me to reflect on how much energy teaching takes and, importantly, who is expending more effort in the learning process—the teacher or the student. This reframing reinforced the importance of pedagogical approaches that deliberately shift cognitive effort back onto learners, encouraging greater ownership and engagement.

I now demonstrate an acute awareness of the contextual challenges faced by apprentice learners, particularly concerns around limited time for study outside of the workplace. This led to productive discussions around the principles of "Learning in the Flow of Work" and how teaching design can better align with real-world professional practice. In this context, we explored flipped learning approaches, including my use of PowerPoint screen recording to create independent, asynchronous learning materials that students can access flexibly, supporting autonomy and self-paced learning.

GW was also keen for me to explore problem-based learning pedagogies as a means of framing learning in authentic, applied contexts and deliberately placing responsibility for learning back onto the student. These conversations highlighted how well problem-based approaches align with 21CLD principles by promoting critical thinking, collaboration, and knowledge construction rather than passive content consumption.

Finally, GW demonstrated a strong commitment to developing me as a lecturer as I develop future engineers with robust learning behaviours and transferable 21st century skills. We discussed how the principles of 21CLD—particularly self-regulation, collaboration, problem-solving, and metacognition—can support learners in becoming more reflective, independent, and adaptable professionals. GW's willingness to engage in reflective, evidence-informed pedagogical discussion has had a significant positive influence on my own practice and has reinforced the value of intentional learning design in preparing students for both academic success and the demands of the modern workplace.